  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge is moving over SD today, which is helping to \npush a bit of high pressure over the state. This high pressure will \nmove some more warm and dry air into SD today. Temperatures today \nand Monday will be warmer than normal, in the 50s to mid 60s. The \ndrier air aloft will help to keep precipitation from developing over \nthe next few days, even with a few frontal boundaries moving through \nthe state. Speaking of boundaries, there will be a weak cold front \nmoving through SD tonight into Monday morning. While this cold front \nwon't dramatically change temperatures overnight, it will help \ncooler air to move in over the period of a few days and will cool \ntemperatures slightly. Monday will be 10-20 degrees warmer than \nnormal and Tuesday 10-15 degrees warmer than normal, then Wednesday \nand Thursday will be around normal to about 10 degrees warmer than \nnormal. \n\nAn upper-level trough will be moving over SD Tuesday night into \nWednesday. This will help to push a surface low pressure over the \nNorthern Plains into MN Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, with \nthe low acting a bit like a clipper system. The models do vary the \nlocation of this low, as the Canadian model has it moving over \nwestern and central SD and other models have it moving from ND to \nMN. In addition to the low's track, the models also vary whether \nthere will be precipitation moving in along with the low and if that \nprecipitation will move over central or northeastern SD. Because of \nthe variability in the models, as well as lower amounts of moisture \nin the atmosphere, there is low confidence in rain showers \ndeveloping over the area. There is a 10-15% chance for isolated rain \nshowers to develop over northeastern SD Tuesday evening into the \nearly nighttime hours, with higher chances to the east over MN.",
